> there to the actual game directory. The same should work on
> any single-user platform where the playground directory will
> not get hard-coded to the binaries.

> With the Linux version, I just first start the game in wizard
> mode to check out compatibility before I continue a real game
> that I started before the changes.

I'll admit that I'm a bit surprised by all this.  As a software
developer, I know that the first thing to do before trying any
new software (and especially anything I compiled myself) is to
make a backup of anything it might touch or use (including write
protected files which it should only read).

For nethack under Unix (my platforms), I'll generally just
rename nethackdir to nethackdir.old, then install.  Folling the
installation *and* tests, I'll copy back anything from
nethackdir.old that I wanted to keep: records, bones, etc.

